📘 OptiShine: Technical Architecture & System Logic
This document presents detailed technical solutions implemented in the OptiShine system, from raw data processing to intelligent AI response mechanisms.

1. Scraper & Sync Logic
Instead of using traditional HTML Crawl methods that are prone to errors when interfaces change, the system uses direct connection through the Zendesk Help Center API.
Data Extraction: Only articles in published status are retrieved to ensure accuracy.
Markdown Conversion: Uses markdownify to convert HTML to Markdown. This removes excess CSS/JS code while preserving semantic structure (headers #, ##, bold **, etc.), helping the Embedding model work more efficiently.
Delta Detection (MD5 Hashing): Each article is assigned an MD5 hash code based on its content. The system compares this hash with old data in scrape_metadata.json. If the hash is unchanged, the system skips processing to save time and avoid unnecessary OpenAI API calls.

2. Advanced Chunking Logic
This is the most critical step for optimizing RAG. Rather than splitting by fixed character count, we split by Document Structure (Recursive Heading Splitting).
Smart Break Points: The system identifies ## (Heading 2) tags as chunk boundaries. This ensures each chunk contains a complete subsection of the guide.
Metadata Tagging: Each chunk automatically inserts information at the beginning: Source: [Article Title] | URL: [Original Link]. This ensures that even when viewed independently, the text passage carries full context for AI.
Quantity Optimization: With 100 articles, the system generates 461 chunks, ensuring each segment is short enough that AI isn't confused by information overload, yet long enough to contain a complete meaning.

3. Main Orchestrator Logic
The main.py file serves as the "Orchestrator" (Conductor) connecting independent components:
Initialization: Establishes connections with OpenAI Assistant and Vector Store.
State Comparison: Reads existing url_map.json to identify which files already exist on OpenAI.
Flow Processing: If an article is identified as "new" or "needs update," it goes through the process: Scrape → Chunk → Upload → Attach to Vector Store.
Cleanup: Deletes intermediate files on the virtual machine to ensure security and optimize memory.

4. Auto Update Daily Job Logic
The system operates without manual intervention thanks to GitHub Actions CI/CD.
Trigger (Cron Job): Configuration 0 0 * * * causes the system to automatically run at 0:00 UTC every day.
Secure Environment: API keys are managed through GitHub Secrets (OOPS), ensuring sensitive information doesn't leak into code.
Persistence (State Storage): After the job completes, the virtual machine executes git commit and git push back to the repo. This ensures metadata files (url_map.json, scrape_metadata.json) are always updated for the next day's run.

5. Response Logic
This logic is configured in the Assistant's System Prompt to guide behavior:
Strict Context: AI is only allowed to answer based on the 461 existing chunks. If no information is found, the Bot must respond "I don't have that information" rather than guessing.
Citation Mapping: When information is found, AI retrieves the File ID of that chunk. The front-end uses this File ID to cross-reference with url_map.json to display the OptiSigns URL link directly below the answer.

6. Front-end Configuration (UI/UX Logic)
The Streamlit application manages the user interface according to these principles:
Chat History Persistence: Uses st.session_state to store conversation history, allowing users to ask follow-up questions while AI remembers previous context.
Streaming UI: Responses are displayed as "Streaming" (character by character) creating a smooth feeling and reducing psychological latency for users.
Markdown Rendering: Supports displaying guides as Lists, Bold text, or Code Blocks so users can easily follow technical steps.
Link daily job logs: https://github.com/shine1904/oopsteaShine/actions



